  /* ── D54 Chain rule ────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  'chain-rule': {
    family: 'panels',
    title: 'Gears in a chain — z = f(g(x)), and how blame multiplies backward',
    frames: [
      { msg: 'A computational graph: x feeds gear g (square it), whose output u feeds gear f (triple it). Run x = 3 forward.',
        panels: [
          { title: 'forward pass', rows: [ { t: 'x = 3', s: 'hot' }, { t: 'u = g(x) = x² = 9' }, { t: 'z = f(u) = 3u = 27' } ] },
        ] },
      { msg: 'The question every optimizer asks: if I nudge x a little, how much does z move? That ratio is dz/dx — and we can get it gear by gear.',
        panels: [
          { title: 'forward pass', rows: [ { t: 'x = 3' }, { t: 'u = 9' }, { t: 'z = 27' } ] },
          { title: 'the question', rows: [ { t: 'nudge x by 0.01 → z moves by ?', s: 'hot' } ] },
        ] },
      { msg: 'Each gear only knows its LOCAL ratio. Gear g: du/dx = 2x = 6 (near x=3, u moves 6× as fast as x). Gear f: dz/du = 3, always.',
        panels: [
          { title: 'gear g: u = x²', rows: [ { t: 'du/dx = 2x = 6', s: 'hot' } ] },
          { title: 'gear f: z = 3u', rows: [ { t: 'dz/du = 3', s: 'hot' } ] },
        ], caption: 'no gear sees the whole chain — only its own teeth' },
      { msg: 'The chain rule: ratios along a chain MULTIPLY. dz/dx = dz/du × du/dx = 3 × 6 = 18. Nudge x by 0.01 and z moves by ~0.18.',
        panels: [
          { title: 'chain rule', rows: [ { t: 'dz/dx = dz/du × du/dx' }, { t: '= 3 × 6 = 18', s: 'hot' } ] },
        ],
        log: ['check: x=3.01 → u=9.0601 → z=27.1803 ≈ 27 + 18×0.01'],
        predict: { q: 'If one gear\'s local gradient were 0, what is dz/dx through that path?', a: '0 — the chain is broken', b: 'whatever the other gears say', t: true } },
      { msg: 'A zero anywhere kills the whole product — that\'s a "dead" path, and exactly what vanishing gradients feel like in deep nets.',
        panels: [
          { title: 'chain of gears', rows: [ { t: 'dz/dx = 3 × 0 × 6 = 0', s: 'hot' }, { t: 'no signal reaches x' } ] },
        ] },
      { msg: 'Backprop (Day 86) is nothing more than this: walk the computational graph backward, multiplying local gradients along every path. One rule, applied a billion times.',
        panels: [
          { title: 'local gradients', rows: [ { t: 'each node: one small derivative' } ] },
          { title: 'backward walk', rows: [ { t: 'multiply along each path', s: 'hot' }, { t: 'sum where paths merge' } ] },
        ] },
    ],
  },

